{"term":"DNA","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:b85c233fc9db0172ba","definition":"Initialism of deoxyribonucleic acid (“a nucleic acid found in all living things (and some non-living things such as certain viruses) which consists of two polynucleotide chains that coil around each other to form a double helix; encoded in its structure are genetic instructions for development, functioning, growth, and reproduction”).","example":"Among the various fractions isolated was one presumed to contain only desoxyribosenucleic acid (DNA). Because of certain speculations on the role of nucleic acids in protein synthesis it was necessary to measure and compare the rate of phosphate entry into DNA with that in other fractions of yeast phosphate.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["thread of life"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"abbreviation, alt-of, initialism","register":"abbreviation, alt-of, countable, initialism, uncountable","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/DNA#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:abec33caa97bd31527","definition":"The part of a living thing that carries genetic information.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"broadly, informal","register":"broadly, countable, informal, uncountable","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/DNA#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:925e95c96fe1d864dc","definition":"The fundamental nature or values of a person, or an organization or other thing, especially when considered as innate or immutable.","example":"These ingredients in a company's DNA mean that [the] company will attract and grow leaders with these qualities.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"countable, figuratively, uncoun